# TEMPLATE — cargo NEVER reads this file. It reads `.cargo/config.toml` (or
# `.cargo/config`), and this repo's `.gitignore` ignores that live file precisely
# so a local override can never be committed. To use this: `cp
# .cargo/config.toml.example .cargo/config.toml`, then adjust the path.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. `src-tauri/Cargo.toml` depends on `fulcrum-harness` as a
# crates.io VERSION PIN (`fulcrum-harness = "0.1.0"`), and the crate's sources are
# NOT in this checkout (they live in https://github.com/archimedes-codes/fulcrum
# and are published FROM there). So editing the engine means editing a file you
# cannot see from here. If you are working on the desktop AND the engine at the
# same time, this redirects the CRATES.IO source at a sibling checkout, so
# `cargo build` compiles YOUR engine code and a change you make there shows up in
# the desktop's build immediately.
#
# ⚠️ THE TABLE NAME CHANGED WHEN THE DEPENDENCY DID, AND THE OLD NAME IS NOW THE
# SILENT FAILURE. The table names the SOURCE being overridden, and the source is
# no longer a git URL — it is the registry's canonical source id, `crates-io`.
# So the table is `[patch.crates-io]`. There is NO URL to match any more, and any
# instruction to spell `[patch."https://github.com/archimedes-codes/fulcrum"]`
# character-for-character is dead: obey it today and cargo does NOT fail. It
# prints `warning: patch … was not used in the crate graph`, exits 0, and builds
# the PUBLISHED crate while you edit your own tree — the exact shape this file
# exists to prevent. Measured on this tree: that stale git-URL table exited 0 and
# cargo added
#     [[patch.unused]]
#     name = "fulcrum-harness"
#     version = "0.1.0"
# to `Cargo.lock`. THAT STANZA IS THE TELL — `grep patch.unused Cargo.lock` after
# any patch experiment. Cargo even appends `help: perhaps you meant one of the
# following: crates-io`, which is the cheapest fix in the file to miss.
#
# THE POLARITY FLIPPED, SO HERE ARE ALL THREE STALE SHAPES, MEASURED. Two of them
# are loud and one is not; the one that is not is the one to fear.
#
#   | your config                                        | exit | cargo says      |
#   |----------------------------------------------------|------|-----------------|
#   | `[patch."https://…/fulcrum"] fulcrum-harness = {…}` |  0   | `warning: patch … was not used in the crate graph` + a `[[patch.unused]]` stanza in `Cargo.lock` |
#   | `[patch.crates-io] fulcrum-core = {…}` (old key)     | 101  | `error: patch location … does not contain packages matching \`fulcrum-core\`` |
#   | `[patch.crates-io] fulcrum-harness = {…}` (correct)  |  0   | nothing — it applies |
#
# So a stale KEY under the right table fails loudly, and a stale TABLE with the
# right key passes silently. If you kept a `.cargo/config.toml` from before the
# rename, assume the silent row until you have checked.
#
# WHY `[patch]` AND NOT `[paths]`. Cargo's older `[paths]` override is deprecated.
# The reason this file used to give for preferring `[patch]` — that `[paths]` only
# rewrites PATH dependencies and "silently does nothing to a pinned git dep" — was
# true of a GIT source and is FALSE of the registry source this is now: measured,
# `[paths]` DOES override a registry dep (`cargo metadata` reports
# `source: None` with a `[paths]` entry pointing at the sibling, exactly as
# `[patch]` does). So `[patch]` is not chosen because `[paths]` cannot do the job.
# It is chosen because it is what cargo documents for overriding a registry
# source, because it is SCOPED to the one named source rather than being a bare
# list of directories that applies wherever a name matches, and because this repo
# already ships it. Do not re-argue this from `[paths]`'s limits.
#
# ⚠️ THE SIBLING CHECKOUT MUST CARRY A VERSION THAT SATISFIES THE REQUIREMENT.
# This half survives the switch of source and it is still the SILENT one:
# `fulcrum-harness = "0.1.0"` means `^0.1.0`, so `[patch]` only applies if the
# patched manifest satisfies it. Point it at a checkout on any version outside the
# requirement — a pre-bump `0.0.x`, or a `0.2.0` you have not adopted — and cargo
# prints
#     warning: patch `fulcrum-harness v0.9.0 (/path/to/fulcrum)` was not used in the crate graph
# and then BUILDS THE PUBLISHED CRATE ANYWAY — exit 0, no error, and your engine
# edits are simply not in the binary. Measured on this tree with a stand-in
# sibling at `0.9.0`: exit 0, exactly that warning, and a `[[patch.unused]]`
# stanza for `fulcrum-harness v0.9.0` in `Cargo.lock`. The warning is not
# informational, it is the whole failure — read it. Fix: check the sibling out at
# a commit whose `Cargo.toml` carries a satisfying `version`, or carry one while
# you work there.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS FOR LOCAL WORK ONLY — NEVER COMMIT THE LIVE VERSION.
# A committed patch would (a) break every other machine, which has no `../fulcrum`
# directory, with `error: patch location \`<path>\` does not contain packages
# matching \`fulcrum-harness\`` (exit 101 — measured both for a path that does not
# exist and for one that exists but is not a crate; the OLD sentence this file
# used to quote, `failed to load source for dependency`, is what a bad GIT source
# printed and it no longer prints), and (b) defeat the pin: the build would use
# whatever that tree happens to hold instead of the published, checksummed
# release. That is the exact failure this pin exists to prevent, so `.gitignore`
# ignores `.cargo/config.toml` and the pin stays the only thing that reaches CI.
#
# HOW TO TELL IT IS ACTUALLY WORKING. `cargo tree -i fulcrum-harness` prints the
# patched source as a PATH, e.g.
#     fulcrum-harness v0.1.0 (/path/to/fulcrum)
# and when the patch is NOT in effect it prints the bare
#     fulcrum-harness v0.1.0
# with NO source suffix at all — that is how a registry dep prints unpatched, so
# do not go looking for a URL there (the old instruction to expect the
# `git+https://…?tag=…` form died with the git source). Machine-checkable
# version: `cargo metadata`'s `source` field for the package is `null` when
# patched and `"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index"` when not.
# Both measured on this tree.
#
# A SECOND CLEANUP, AND IT NOW APPLIES IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. When the patch DOES
# apply, cargo rewrites `Cargo.lock`: it drops the package's `source` and
# `checksum` lines (measured — exactly those two lines and nothing else). When the
# patch merely FAILS to apply, cargo still rewrites `Cargo.lock`, adding the
# `[[patch.unused]]` stanza above. So `git checkout Cargo.lock` after deleting
# `.cargo/config.toml` is required in BOTH directions now, not only the
# successful one. Verify with `cargo tree -i fulcrum-harness`: it must print the
# bare `fulcrum-harness v0.1.0` again, and `grep patch.unused Cargo.lock` must be
# empty, before you commit anything. Remember the lock IS tracked and the config
# is not.
#
# HOW TO UNDO IT: delete `.cargo/config.toml`, then `git checkout Cargo.lock`.
#
# ⚠️ THE TABLE NAME IS `[patch.crates-io]`, WITH THE DOT. `[patch "crates-io"]`
# (a space, no dot) is NOT valid TOML and cargo dies with
# `could not parse TOML configuration … unclosed table, expected ']'` — measured,
# exit 101, and it is a hard failure of EVERY cargo command in the repo, not a
# silent no-op. The dotted form is what cargo documents: the string after the dot
# is a KEY of the `[patch]` table naming the source being overridden.

[patch.crates-io]
fulcrum-harness = { path = "../fulcrum" }
